  "page1": {
    "title": "Heat on the Move",
    "subtitle": "How heat travels from warm things to cool things every day.",
    "body": "Touch a warm cup of cocoa. Your hand feels hot. This is <u>heat</u> moving to you.\n\nHeat is the energy in warm things. It is not the same as temperature. Temperature tells us how hot or cold a thing is.\n\nHeat always moves from a hot thing to a cold thing. A warm spoon in cold soup gets cold. The soup gets a little warm.\n\nIn metal, heat moves fast. We <mark>call this conduction</mark>. The metal pan gets hot, and your soup cooks.\n\nWood and air move heat slowly. So we hold a hot pot with a wood spoon. Our hand stays safe.\n\nHeat never stops moving all around us.",
    "illustration": "assets/illustrations/mars/2026-07/01.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "Heat moving from a warm cup to a cold hand, and along a metal spoon in soup."
  },
